	<div id=3D"contentcenter">
		<h3>Apple Power Macintosh G4 1.25 DP (MDD)  Specs</h3>
		<p class=3D"specstop">Identifiers: Mirrored Drive Doors - M8573LL/A - <a =
class=3D"ital" href=3D"https://everymac.com/ultimate-mac-lookup/?identify=
=3DPowerMac3,6">PowerMac3,6</a> - <a class=3D"ital" href=3D"https://everyma=
c.com/ultimate-mac-lookup/?identify=3DM8570">M8570</a> -  <a class=3D"ital"=
 href=3D"https://everymac.com/ultimate-mac-lookup/?identify=3D1914">1914</a=
></p>

	    <p><em><a href=3D"https://everymac.com/systems/apple/powermac_g4/">All=
 Power Macintosh G4 Models</a> | <a href=3D"https://everymac.com/systems/by=
_year/macs-released-in-2002.html">All 2002 Models</a> | <a href=3D"https://=
everymac.com/ultimate-mac-comparison-chart/?compare=3Dall-powerpc-macs&amp;=
highlight=3D0&amp;prod1=3DPowerMacG4024" target=3D"_top">Dynamically Compar=
e This Mac to Others</a></em></p>

	=09

<p></p><br>
=09
	<p><img src=3D"https://everymac.com/images/cpu_pictures/apple_powermac_g4_=
mdd.jpg" alt=3D"Apple Power Mac G4" width=3D"83" height=3D"180" class=3D"co=
mppic">The Apple Power Macintosh G4/1.25 DP (Mirrored Drive Doors) features=
 dual 1.25 GHz PowerPC 7455 (G4) processors each with the AltiVec "Velocity=
 Engine" vector processing unit, 256k "on chip" level 2 cache, and 2 MB of =
DDR SRAM level 3 backside cache. It shipped configured with 512 MB of RAM, =
a 120 GB Ultra ATA/100 hard drive, a 2X DVD-R/CD-RW "SuperDrive", and a 4X =
AGP ATI Radeon 9000 Pro graphics card with 64 MB of DDR SDRAM. AirPort (802=
.11b) was available by custom configuration.<br><br>The Power Macintosh G4 =
(Mirrored Drive Doors) series, so dubbed by Apple because of the "mirror fi=
nish" on the drive bay doors -- but often called "Wind Tunnel" by users ref=
erring to its noise -- was based on the architecture used by the <a href=3D=
"https://everymac.com/systems/apple/xserve/">Xserve G4</a>. Compared to the=
 "Quicksilver 2002" series that it replaced, the Power Macintosh G4 "Mirror=
ed Drive Doors" models all add dual processors with level 3 caches and an a=
nalog audio input jack. The low-end <a href=3D"https://everymac.com/systems=
/apple/powermac_g4/specs/powermac_g4_867_dp_mdd.html">Power Macintosh G4/86=
7 DP (MDD)</a> uses faster PC2100 DDR memory and the higher-end <a href=3D"=
https://everymac.com/systems/apple/powermac_g4/specs/powermac_g4_1.0_dp_mdd=
.html">Power Macintosh G4/1.0 DP (MDD)</a> and <a href=3D"https://everymac.=
com/systems/apple/powermac_g4/specs/powermac_g4_1.25_dp_mdd.html">Power Mac=
intosh G4/1.25 DP (MDD)</a> have faster 167 MHz system buses, use faster st=
ill PC2700 DDR memory, and have superior graphics cards as well.<br><br>Lik=
e all previous Power Macintosh G4 models, the "Mirrored Drive Doors" line u=
ses a case design effectively the same as that introduced with the <a href=
=3D"https://everymac.com/systems/apple/powermac_g3/">Power Macintosh G3 (Bl=
ue &amp; White)</a> -- complete with a fold down door on the side that make=
s upgrading very easy -- but it is silver like the "Quicksilver" models and=
 adds the aforementioned mirrored drive bay doors along with four decorativ=
e indentions on the front reminiscent of the "Ventiports" on a <a href=3D"h=
ttp://auto.howstuffworks.com/1949-buick-roadmaster.htm" target=3D"_blank">1=
949 Buick Roadmaster</a>.</p>
	   =20
	<p><em>Despite the age of this system, site sponsor <a rel=3D"nofollow" hr=
ef=3D"https://everymac.com/owc" target=3D"_blank">Other World Computing</a>=
  has RAM, hard drives, SSDs, and other upgrades available for <a rel=3D"no=
follow" href=3D"https://eshop.macsales.com/MyOWC/Upgrades.cfm?model=3D171" =
target=3D"_blank">this specific Mac</a> while supplies last.</em></p>
